{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788188150,"nonce":"SZ8WB1w5nmMOPPHSlVkav8EllTk=","message":"Built on branch `spec/auto-arm-lever` (3 commits). Release build clean, 0 warnings 0 errors. Not run in-game — the orchestrator owns acceptance; `accept/auto-arm-lever.md` has the envelopes.\n\nWHERE THE ISSUE'S API NOTES DIFFER FROM FSWA'S SOURCE. Trusted the source in each case.\n\n1. `SeekVerbs.cs` DOES NOT EXIST on `main` or on any branch in this repo. The issue and the dispatch both name it as the in-repo template for a mod-aware verb; it is still in a parallel worktree. `Source/AutoRimmer/FswaBridge.cs` was therefore written from the review lessons in the issue text rather than from the file, and the two will want reconciling into one shared reflection helper at merge.\n\n2. \"check whether SetAutoArm is one of the sites MpSync wraps.\" It is, and the shape is not what the question implies: `MpSync.RegisterAll` registers `AutoArmTracker.SetAutoArm(Pawn, bool)` ITSELF with `MP.RegisterSyncMethod` — deliberately, because both of FSWA's callers (the gizmo's toggleAction and `PawnColumnWorker_AutoArm.SetValue`) funnel through that one static setter. There is no separate synced wrapper to prefer; the direct call IS the synced path. Documented at the call site.\n\n   The caveat that comes with it, which is FSWA's design and not a defect: MP's prefix only fires in interface context (`Multiplayer.ShouldSync` requires `InInterface`). AutoRimmer's verbs run from `AgentGameComponent.GameComponentUpdate`, which is not, so under Multiplayer this write would execute locally and not ship. Out of scope for a single-player bench; recorded in DESIGN.md's decisions log so it is not rediscovered. Relevant to FSWA's own e82e399.\n\n3. THE GATE HAS TWO WIDGETS AND THE ISSUE CITES ONE. The issue names the gizmo (`Command_ToggleAutoArm`, SidearmGizmos.cs). The gate that actually matters for a verb reproducing the Assign tab is `PawnColumnWorker_AutoArm.HasCheckbox` — `pawn.IsColonist \u0026\u0026 !pawn.WorkTagIsDisabled(WorkTags.Violent)` — a file the issue does not mention at all. The gizmo (HarmonyPatches.cs `Patch_PawnGizmos.Postfix`) is drawn for `IsColonistPlayerControlled || MechUtility.IsWeaponUsableMech`, and is `Disable`d rather than hidden for a non-mechanoid incapable of violence. So a weapon-capable PLAYER MECH has a route the column does not offer, and it skips the Violent check. The lever honours the union and cites both; enforcing only the column would have invented a restriction the player does not have.\n\n4. A THIRD GATE IS NOT A WIDGET AT ALL. `SetAutoArm` returns SILENTLY unless `MpSync.Configurable(pawn)` holds (`pawn != null \u0026\u0026 !pawn.Dead \u0026\u0026 !pawn.Destroyed`). Pre-checked, so a dead pawn is refused with a reason instead of reported applied against a write that never happened.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED\n- `assign {pawns:[…], auto_arm:true|false}`, composing with the other eight levers, in `levers`, on the same journal row.\n- Every write read back from the tracker; a disagreement is a REJECTION with a diagnosis (`WriteDiagnosis()` probes `AutoArmTracker.Get`, which is what the setter's own first clause tests), never a success. A read-back that cannot be made at all reports the state as UNKNOWN, not unchanged.\n- Absent mod refuses ONE LEVER by name; the rest of the call still applies.\n- `auto_arm` echoed in before/after on EVERY assign call, and in `pawn {sections:[\"area\"]}` — the tab section where the checkbox lives. Both `true`/`false`/`null`; null is \"could not look\", never \"off\".\n- Reflection binds by signature (param types + IsStatic + return type + typed delegate); drift disables the lever at load with a journaled warning rather than throwing at a call site.\n\nNOT VERIFIED, and only the orchestrator can\n- Anything at runtime. No envelope has been sent; the type name `FSWA.AutoArmTracker` resolving under `AccessTools.TypeByName` on the live bench is itself unproven.\n- The weapon-capable mech branch: needs Fortified Framework / DMS and a mech implementing `Fortified.IWeaponUsable`. Failure mode if wrong is a named refusal, not a bad write.\n- The absent-mod path: FSWA is in the bench profile, so it needs a ModsConfig edit and a restart. Phase 5 of the accept note, marked optional.\n- Phase 3.2 (a colonist incapable of Violence) is not stageable with any `dev:*` verb that ships. Skip with a NOTE if the colony has none.\n\nTHE STANDING ORDER is unchanged and still owed by 4.2: raider drops land forbidden, `FSWA_MapComponent` skips forbidden weapons, and a direct `equip` bypasses forbidden — so the manual path works while the autonomous one silently does nothing. Phase 4.5 of the accept note unforbids explicitly for that reason.","files":null}]}